# Pool extras — packages that ship in the apt pool on the installer
# ISO but are NEVER installed in any chroot (live, installer ramdisk,
# or target debootstrap).  Available to apt at install time and
# post-install via the cdrom: source.
#
# Contract:
#   1. Each entry is resolved by `dependencytree.resolve_packages` in
#      Pass VII (see scripts/build.py) the same way pkg.list /
#      live.list / installer.list deb-arm entries are.  All transitive
#      Depends are pulled in and end up in `selected_pkgs` → built by
#      the source-build pipeline → land in `repo/` → ship in
#      `staging/pool/`.
#   2. Pass VII calls resolve_packages with `check_conflicts=False`
#      and `validate_selection` skips Breaks/Conflicts where either
#      side is in `pool_extras_pkg_names`.  This lets mutually-
#      conflicting packages (e.g. `grub-pc` + `grub-efi-amd64`)
#      coexist in the pool — apt on the target enforces conflicts at
#      install time, picking exactly one.
#   3. `cmd_build_iso_installer` subtracts pool extras from
#      `base_include` so debootstrap does NOT install them on the
#      target.  They stay in `_pool_whitelist` so the .debs ship in
#      the cdrom pool.
#
# What this is for:
#   - Mutually-exclusive packages where install-time selection
#     happens on the target (not at build time).  Canonical example:
#     bootloader metas where grub-installer picks `grub-pc` (BIOS) or
#     `grub-efi-amd64` (EFI) based on firmware mode.
#   - Optional packages we want available on a fresh install without
#     network access — operator can `apt-get install <name>` from the
#     cdrom source post-install.
#
# History (2026-05-13 / -14):
#   - Phase D (commit 875200e) swapped `grub-pc` (META) for
#     `grub-pc-bin` + `grub-efi-amd64-bin` in `pkg.list` so an EFI
#     install would have both `-bin` packages available.  Side
#     effect: the META .debs (built by the grub2 source build, so
#     present in `repo/`) stopped getting copied into
#     `staging/pool/` because `_pool_whitelist = selected_pkgs -
#     live_excl` no longer included them.  Both BIOS
#     (`apt-install grub-pc`) and EFI (`apt-install grub-efi-amd64`)
#     `grub-installer` runs failed with "no installation candidate".
#   - First fix attempt (the file existed in an earlier shape) was a
#     manifest of names whose `.deb`s already happened to be in
#     `repo/` — bypassed the resolver entirely.  Worked for the grub
#     case but had no path to add packages whose source we hadn't
#     built yet (e.g. open-vm-tools).  Redesigned 2026-05-14 to go
#     through the resolver with conflicts disabled — same dep
#     resolution as everything else in the build, plus the conflict
#     bypass for genuinely-pool-only entries.

# ─── Bootloader metas ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# grub-installer apt-installs whichever fits the target's firmware
# mode at install time.  The Conflict between them is enforced by
# apt on the target — both .debs sit indexed in /cdrom/pool side by
# side here.
grub-pc
grub-efi-amd64
# NOTE: shim-signed is intentionally OMITTED — Athena has no Secure Boot
# story shipped today.  grub-installer's "Additionally installing
# shim-signed" step soft-fails (non-fatal); install completes on
# Secure-Boot-disabled machines.  On machines with Secure Boot mandatorily
# enabled, the user must disable it in firmware setup until SECBOOT-01
# (self-signed + MOK enrollment, see docs/plans/secboot-01-mok-self-sign.md)
# lands.  We deliberately do NOT tunnel Debian's shim-signed chain because
# it pins a +debNuN-versioned grub2 (cascade) and ties Athena to Debian's
# signing trust — that's a downstream-of-Debian posture we want to avoid.

# ─── VMware guest tooling ──────────────────────────────────────────
# `finish-install`'s `hw-detect` runs `apt-install open-vm-tools` on
# VMware hypervisors.  Available in the pool here so that probe
# succeeds on VMware targets (no-op on bare-metal / non-VMware
# hypervisors).  Source builds normally — small dependency closure
# (libdrm2, libmspack0, libtirpc3, libxmlsec1, ethtool, zerofree on
# top of what we already ship).
open-vm-tools
open-vm-tools-desktop

# ─── Console keymap configuration ──────────────────────────────────
# `base-installer`'s `apt-install` queues `console-setup` for /target
# so the installed system has a working console keymap matching the
# locale picked at install time.  Without this in the pool,
# base-installer logs `Failed to install console-setup into
# /target/: 100` (verified 2026-05-18 install).  Not pre-installed
# in pkg.list because the live image doesn't need it — target-side
# only.
#
# History: removed 2026-05-17 in a pool-audit cleanup with the
# justification "reaches transitively via kbd".  That was correct
# for keyboard-configuration + xkb-data (kbd → keyboard-configuration
# → xkb-data) — they still reach the pool transitively today.  But
# it was WRONG for console-setup: base-installer apt-installs
# console-setup as a SEPARATE step on /target, independent of the
# kbd dep chain.  Re-added 2026-05-18 after the install failure.
console-setup

# ─── FORK-01 Step 5 follow-up: tasksel task availability ─────────────
# Each section below makes a kept tasksel task's hard Depends
# reachable in /cdrom/pool so apt-install at install time succeeds
# when the operator selects the task.  Our fork (athena-tasksel-data,
# coming in next sub-step) ships the .desc that maps task → these
# package sets.  None of these are installed by default — only when
# the matching task is ticked in tasksel's UI.

# ─── Debian "standard" task — Priority: standard packages ────────────
# Replicates Debian's `Packages: standard` semantic so apt-get can
# satisfy the standard task selection.  87 Priority: standard pkgs
# total (4 already in our explicit lists); 83 added here.
#
# Our own athena-tasksel-data fork's `standard` task will use a
# CURATED Key: subset (~30 of the most-useful ones) rather than the
# blanket `Packages: standard` sigil — so default install stays
# trim, but operator who wants the full Debian-standard experience
# can `apt install <name>` for any of these post-install.
#
# DEBIAN RESIDUE NOTE: `debian-faq`, `doc-debian`, `reportbug`,
# `python3-reportbug` are Debian-specific (FAQ docs, BTS-submission
# tooling).  They are Priority: standard so they ship in pool.  They
# are already PUBLISHED on the mirror (live coord claims), so they stay
# selected here — dropping a published package leaves orphaned claims +
# a CRITICAL `claim_not_in_apt_index` audit (no withdraw/retract flow
# exists yet).  Strip Debian identity by FORKING (asgard-reportbug etc.):
# a fork claim cleanly supersedes the upstream claim; deletion does not.
apt-listchanges
bind9-dnsutils
bind9-host
dbus-system-bus
debian-faq
dh-sequence-bash-completion
dnsutils
doc-debian
host
inetutils-telnet
libansicolor-perl
libarchive-tar-perl
libattribute-handlers-perl
libautodie-perl
libcompress-zlib-perl
libcpan-meta-perl
libdigest-md5-perl
libdigest-perl
libextutils-command-perl
libextutils-install-perl
libextutils-parsexs-perl
libfile-spec-perl
libi18n-langtags-perl
libio-compress-base-perl
libio-compress-bzip2-perl
libio-compress-zlib-perl
libio-zlib-perl
liblocale-maketext-perl
liblocale-maketext-simple-perl
liblockfile-bin
libmath-complex-perl
libmime-base64-perl
libmodule-load-perl
libnet-ping-perl
libparams-check-perl
libparent-perl
libparse-cpan-meta-perl
libperl-ostype-perl
libpod-escapes-perl
libstorable-perl
libsys-syslog-perl
libthread-queue-perl
libthreads-perl
libthreads-shared-perl
libtime-hires-perl
libtime-piece-perl
libversion-requirements-perl
man
mime-support
netcat
netcat-traditional
podlators-perl
python3-reportbug
reportbug
telnet
telnet-client
wget

# ─── Debian "laptop" task — hard Depends + key Recommends ────────────
# task-laptop pulls `anacron` (Depends) + bluetooth, powertop, iw,
# wireless-tools, wpasupplicant, avahi-autoipd (Recommends).  Recs
# would be silently skipped if missing from pool, but most laptops
# expect them — ship all in pool.  Operator picks "laptop" task and
# all install (or laptop-detect auto-flags it).
anacron
avahi-autoipd
bluetooth
iw
powertop
wireless-tools

# ─── Debian "desktop" task — hard Depends + key Recommends ───────────
# task-desktop pulls xorg + xserver-xorg-* drivers + desktop-base
# (Debian theme — see TODO) + key system services (cups for
# printing, alsa-utils for audio, avahi+mdns for network discovery,
# xdg-utils for desktop integration, fonts-symbola for emoji
# fallback).  task-gnome-desktop further Recommends-on top of these
# via task-desktop's chain.
#
# TODO (folds into FORK-01 Step 7 athena-branding): replace
# `desktop-base` with Athena-branded theme/wallpaper package via
# Provides+Conflicts+Replaces or dpkg-divert.  Today's
# desktop-base ships Debian-themed wallpaper, GRUB theme, plymouth
# splash.  Tracked in fork-source.md plan.
alsa-utils
cups
fonts-symbola
xorg

# ─── Asgard operator-facing metapackages ──────────────────────────
# Empty-payload `.deb`s whose Depends: list is a curated group.
# Ship in the pool so operators can `apt install asgard-<group>` on
# a booted system and pull the whole group atomically.  Built from
# fork/source/asgard-<group>/.  Pattern B per docs/branding-
# methodology.md — coexists with the pkg.list group + tasksel Key
# list, replaces neither.
#
# Hierarchy: asgard-gnome-desktop is the base.  Every subcategory
# metapackage below Depends on asgard-gnome-desktop so installing
# any subcategory pulls the full GNOME base automatically — a
# single `apt install asgard-office` brings up "GNOME + office".
asgard-gnome-desktop
# Subcategories — alphabetical
asgard-accessibility
asgard-gnome-extras
asgard-multimedia
asgard-network-services
asgard-office
asgard-pim
asgard-printing-extras

# ─── Non-free firmware + CPU microcode (non-free-firmware component) ──
# Curated subset of Debian's non-free-firmware, TUNNELED (prebuilt binaries,
# see [Source] Tunneled in build.conf — sources intel-microcode /
# amd64-microcode / firmware-nonfree).  Available in the pool so hw-detect's
# install-time firmware machinery (post-base-installer.d/50install-firmware +
# finish-install.d/08hw-detect) can apt-install the matching microcode/firmware
# for the target's hardware.  These ship in the pool and publish under the
# non-free-firmware component; NOT pre-installed in any chroot.
#   microcode — apt-installed by hw-detect for the matching CPU vendor
intel-microcode
amd64-microcode
#   Wi-Fi / NIC firmware (the common "no network after install" devices)
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-realtek
firmware-atheros
firmware-brcm80211
#   GPU + broad misc device firmware
firmware-amd-graphics
firmware-misc-nonfree
